# Sunwave fingerprint sensor: gesture key layout
#
# The fingerprint reader is not just a reader: the `sunwave-fp` driver (on
# spi0.0, even though the device tree node is called `mediatek,goodix-fp` --
# a dual-vendor board) registers an `sf-keys` input device that reports the
# gestures made on the sensor.
#
# Without this file `sf-keys` falls back to Generic.kl, which maps 105 and 106
# as DPAD_LEFT/RIGHT: the swipes would end up moving focus inside apps, which
# makes no sense to anyone unaware the sensor does this too. The real actions
# are decided by KeyHandler, which gets the key before the app does.
#
# The gestures are NOT on by default: they are enabled in
# /vendor/etc/sw_config.xml with <navigation>true</navigation>, otherwise the
# HAL reports the tap only. See docs/bringup/hardware-riferimento.md.
#
# What was actually observed, with `getevent -lt` while using the sensor (the
# counts come from a 45-second session):
#
#   HAL "nav Key" -> SF_KEY_F11   -> scancode  68  KEY_F10    162 eventi
#   HAL "nav R"   -> SF_KEY_RIGHT -> scancode 106  KEY_RIGHT   34 eventi
#   HAL "nav L"   -> SF_KEY_LEFT  -> scancode 105  KEY_LEFT    28 eventi
#
# The sensor resolves one axis only: swipes along the phone's long axis are
# classified as taps, not as a direction.

key 68    F10
key 105   DPAD_LEFT
key 106   DPAD_RIGHT

# Declared by the driver in the sf-keys bitmap but never observed coming out.
# They are mapped anyway: should a HAL update or a different configuration make
# them appear, they would already arrive under a sensible name instead of
# falling back to Generic.kl.
key 103   DPAD_UP
key 108   DPAD_DOWN
key 102   HOME
key 158   BACK
key 139   MENU
key 28    DPAD_CENTER
key 143   WAKEUP    WAKE
